    thinking: "**Switching Authentication Methods**\n\nI\'m aiming to remove Google Auth and implement standard username/password login. Currently, the system relies on Firebase Admin to verify Google ID tokens, but that must shift. I\'m focusing on the necessary changes in the Firebase setup and backend to accommodate this new authentication method.\n\n\n**Refining Authentication Strategy**\n\nI\'m now considering a custom authentication system using JWTs with bcrypt for password hashing. However, I\'m questioning if we even need Firebase Auth at all. The user\'s request focuses on username/password login, so JWTs might be simpler than Firebase Email/Password auth. The backend already has a `users` table, which is helpful, and the initial schema included a password hash field. I am now rethinking the approach, as I believe I can move away from Firebase entirely.\n\n\n**Implementing New Login Flow**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation details. I\'ve decided on the next steps: installing `bcryptjs` and `jsonwebtoken`, updating the database schema to include `username` and `passwordHash` while removing `uid`, and creating the necessary migrations. Next, I will modify `server.ts` to handle login and registration with username/password, and issue JWTs. Finally, I will update `middleware/auth.ts` to verify these JWTs.\n\n\n**Adjusting User Interface**\n\nI\'m now shifting focus to the front-end. I\'m updating `AuthContext.tsx` to handle JWT-based login and modifying `Login.tsx` to display a username/password form, with potential for registration. I plan to remove Firebase config and files if they\'re not needed after this, but I am keeping them around just in case. I\'m updating the database schema to include `username` and `passwordHash`.\n\n\n"
